window.docContents[143].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection":{"contents":"The transport solution  is data\nsliding along straight characteristics. For the one-dimensional wave equation\nwith unit wave speed, d'Alembert's formula\n\n\n\nsplits a plucked string into left- and right-moving halves. The\nchecked traveling-sine example\nreuses the sine profile from the heat chapter: translation preserves its\namplitude, while the heat mode decays. Its derivative identities verify both\nthe wave residual and the unit-speed transport equation; the transport example\nalso checks the initial datum. The\ncertified plots of the computation chapter draw the\ntraveling wave  and the Burgers rarefaction profile as bands whose\npanels are kernel-checked enclosure theorems. Burgers's equation  has\ncharacteristics that carry their own speed and, for suitable decreasing initial\ndata, run into each other in finite time. Conservation laws have weak solutions\nthat may contain shocks, governed by the Rankine–Hugoniot condition.\nHamilton–Jacobi equations have continuous solutions that develop creases rather\nthan jumps.\n\nEntropy inequalities do more than narrow the field: in scalar classes with a\ncomparison theorem they pick out exactly one admissible solution, which is the\nbest outcome available once uniqueness of weak solutions has been lost.\n\nFor the classical geometric perspective, see .\n provides a route through the analytic tools: the early chapters for\nclassical wave formulas and nonlinear first-order equations, §7.2 for\nvariational waves, and the later chapters for Hamilton–Jacobi equations,\nconservation laws, and nonlinear waves. \ndevelops the viscosity-solution approach to Hamilton–Jacobi equations.\nFor weak evolution in function spaces, see\n.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations","header":"7.1. Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGive the Laplacian a time direction to act in and you get the heat equation\n. Temperature in a conducting body, the concentration of a\ndiffusing chemical, and the probability density of a Brownian particle all evolve\nby it, and what they have in common is that detail gets destroyed: corners in\nthe initial profile are gone at every positive time. Run the clock backwards and\nthe same mechanism amplifies high frequencies without bound, which is why the\nbackward problem is ill posed on the standard data spaces and why the theory is\nnot symmetric in time.\n\nThe Gaussian convolution solves the equation by differentiation under the\nintegral and attains its initial datum by an approximate-identity limit.\nThe classical route starts with the Gaussian kernel:\nit solves the heat equation at\nevery positive time, its convolution with the data attains the datum as\n, Duhamel's formula absorbs a forcing term, and the heat-ball\nmean-value formula and the maximum and comparison principles follow.\n\nFor weak solutions, Galerkin approximation on a Gelfand triple replaces the\nexplicit formula, and the Lions–Magenes theorem gives the weak solution a\ncontinuous representative in the pivot space. Choosing the triple gives\nexistence and uniqueness for the\nDirichlet heat equation and for divergence-form operators with lower-order\nterms, and a graded family of regularity theorems states which Bochner-space\nconclusion each level of data buys. Convex gradient diffusion and Turing's\ndiffusion-driven instability give a nonlinear and a coupled example. A final\npart asks when diffusion loses and a nonlinear solution ceases to exist in\nfinite time.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"6. Parabolic Equations","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations"}});